Subject: Compose the layers of YUV ColorBuffers through YCbCr samplers

The compositor sampled RGB images only, so the hwcomposer handed every YUV layer (a video's
pictures) back to SurfaceFlinger. SurfaceFlinger then kept a client target the size of the
display (two 3840x3840 RGBA buffers, 112.5 MiB on the host) and drew the whole display on the
GPU for every video frame.

A YUV ColorBuffer (patch 0019) is now composed like any other layer. Its image is read
through an image view and an immutable sampler that share a sampler YCbCr conversion made
from the layer's matrix (BT.601, BT.709, BT.2020), range (full, limited) and chroma order
(U, V or V, U), which the guest puts in the layer's colour: a buffer layer's colour is unused
otherwise. A layer without that description is BT.601, limited range, U then V. Each
conversion has its own descriptor set layout, pipeline layout and pipelines, made the first
time a layer needs them; the views and descriptor sets live for one frame. A YCbCr sampler
clamps to the edge, so a YUV layer's flip and rotation turn about the crop's centre.

The host lists ANDROID_EMU_dsa_yuv_composition when it composes with Vulkan, makes YUV
ColorBuffers and has sampler YCbCr conversion; a guest hands it YUV layers only then.

